---
import DemoLessonLayout from "../../../layouts/DemoLessonLayout.astro";
const createFile =
  'python3 -c \'from pathlib import Path; Path("hello.txt").write_bytes(b"HELLO")\'';
const dockerServer =
  "docker run --rm --name tcp-demo-server --network tcp-demo \\\n" +
  '  --mount "type=bind,src=$PWD,dst=/lesson" -w /lesson python:3.12-slim \\\n' +
  "  python -u server.py --host 0.0.0.0";
const dockerClient =
  "docker run --rm --network tcp-demo \\\n" +
  '  --mount "type=bind,src=$PWD,dst=/lesson,readonly" -w /lesson python:3.12-slim \\\n' +
  "  python client.py hello.txt --host tcp-demo-server";
---

<DemoLessonLayout
  title="Connect two Docker containers"
  description="Send the same sample from a client container to a server container by name."
  downloads
  next={{
    path: "tutorials/tcp-demo/client-dashboard/",
    label: "Plan a client and dashboard",
  }}
>
  <section class="lab-section" aria-labelledby="docker-setup">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <h2 id="docker-setup">Run both containers</h2>
      <p>
        Start Docker first. Use a terminal that supports Bash-style quoting and
        save the two downloads in a dedicated demo folder.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p>
      In the folder with both scripts, pull the image before the demo. This
      exercise creates a dedicated Docker network. Rename any existing
      <code>received-file.bin</code> in the folder before starting.
    </p>
    <pre class="lab-code"><code>{createFile + "\ndocker pull python:3.12-slim\ndocker network create tcp-demo"}</code></pre>
    <p>Start the server container in one terminal.</p>
    <pre
      class="lab-code"
      tabindex="0"
      aria-label="Docker server command"
    ><code>{dockerServer}</code></pre>
    <p>
      In the second terminal, send the file to the server container by name.
    </p>
    <pre
      class="lab-code"
      tabindex="0"
      aria-label="Docker client command"
    ><code>{dockerClient}</code></pre>
    <p>
      The received file appears in your working folder. Both containers share
      the Docker network, so they can talk without publishing a host port. To
      reach the server from the host, publish its port to loopback with{" "}
      <code>-p 127.0.0.1:5001:5001</code> before the image name in the server
      command. The server container exits after one transfer. When finished, run
      <code>docker network rm tcp-demo</code>.
      <a href="https://docs.docker.com/engine/network/drivers/bridge/">
        Docker bridge networking
      </a>
    </p>

    <details>
      <summary>Read the Docker command</summary>
      <ul class="lab-steps">
        <li>
          <code>--network tcp-demo</code> puts both containers on a bridge where
          the client can resolve <code>tcp-demo-server</code> by name.
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>--host 0.0.0.0</code> makes the Python server listen on the
          container's network interfaces. Loopback in a container only reaches
          that same container.
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>--mount</code> lets the container see your demo folder. The
          client mounts it read-only. The server writes the received file there
          so you can inspect it after the container exits.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </details>
    <p>
      <strong>Check your work.</strong> Wait for the server to listen before
      running the client. Check its OK reply and find{" "}
      <code>received-file.bin</code> on the host.
    </p>
  </section>
</DemoLessonLayout>
